                "For decades, digital marketing teams assigned financial credit across multi-channel customer journeys using subjective, rule-based heuristics. The earliest and most primitive was First-Touch Attribution, which allocated one hundred percent of transaction value to the initial touchpoint that introduced the prospect to the brand. While First-Touch acknowledged top-of-funnel discovery, it completely ignored all subsequent consideration, nurturing, retargeting, and closing efforts. Conversely, Last-Touch Attribution assigned total credit to the final link clicked immediately prior to checkout. As established in econometrics, Last-Touch introduced catastrophic distortions, aggressively overfunding branded search and retargeting while starving top-of-funnel awareness channels. Recognizing these extremes, legacy marketing software introduced static compromise heuristics: Linear Attribution, which divided credit equally across all touchpoints, and Position-Based (or U-Shaped) Attribution, which assigned forty percent to the first touch, forty percent to the last touch, and divided the remaining twenty percent equally among intermediate interactions.",
                "While position-based and linear heuristics appeared more balanced on the surface, they were economically defenseless. Every rule-based heuristic suffers from the Fatal Axiom of Subjectivity: the attribution weights are chosen arbitrarily by human committee rather than derived mathematically from the empirical data. Why should the first touch receive exactly forty percent instead of twenty-eight percent or sixty-two percent? Why should a passive display banner ad that was accidentally clicked carry the exact same weight as a thirty-minute in-depth product demonstration webinar? Rule-based models treat all touchpoints within a given position identically, completely oblivious to time decay between touches, channel interaction sequences, touchpoint frequency, and consumer intent transitions.",
                "Furthermore, heuristic attribution models exhibit severe structural vulnerabilities. They suffer from Path Length Bias: as a consumer's journey lengthens from two touchpoints to fifteen touchpoints, linear attribution mechanically dilutes the credit of high-impact nurturing channels to single-digit percentages, incentivizing growth teams to terminate channels that play vital roles in complex B2B buying committees. Additionally, heuristic models create internal corporate conflict: the search agency, the social agency, and the email marketing team each lobby for whichever subjective rule maximizes their specific fee structure. To achieve objective, audited accountability, enterprise marketing science completely abandons rule-based heuristics in favor of algorithmic attribution models rooted in cooperative game theory and stochastic process modeling.",
